# Placement Accuracy Checker Robot Test Guide

This guide explains how to test the placement accuracy checker on the Clearpath robot using a hardcoded planner input.

The checker currently performs the following pipeline:

```text
Robot RGB-D camera
    ↓
AprilTag 16h5 detection inside placement_accuracy_checker.py
    ↓
Current block pose estimated in camera frame
    ↓
Pose transformed into target/world frame using TF
    ↓
Compared against hardcoded planner desired pose
    ↓
Returns good / misplaced / unseen
```

## Current Test Tag

```text
AprilTag family: 16h5
Tag ID: 13
Tag size: 55 mm = 0.055 m
```

## Important Notes

The checker expects the planner’s desired pose and the transformed perceived pose to be in the same frame.

For robot testing, the target frame should usually be either:

```text
world
```

or:

```text
odom
```

Use whichever frame the planner is using for desired block poses.

If the robot TF tree does not contain `world`, use `odom` for the first test.

---

# 1. SSH into the robot

From your laptop:

```bash
ssh robot@192.168.0.20
```

Then on the robot:

```bash
cd ~/aruco_ws
source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
source install/setup.bash
```

---

# 2. Get latest code and build

```bash
cd ~/aruco_ws/src/collective-construction

git checkout placement-accuracy-checker
git pull
```

Build the updated interfaces and checker:

```bash
cd ~/aruco_ws

source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash

colcon build --packages-select cc_interfaces manipulator --symlink-install

source install/setup.bash
```

Confirm the checker executable exists:

```bash
ros2 pkg executables manipulator | grep placement
```

Expected:

```text
manipulator placement_accuracy_checker
```

---

# 3. Confirm camera topics

Run:

```bash
cd ~/aruco_ws
source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
source install/setup.bash

ros2 topic list | grep -E "camera|color|depth|camera_info"
```

On the Clearpath robot, the camera topics may look like this:

```text
/j100_0897/sensors/camera_0/color/image
/j100_0897/sensors/camera_0/depth/image
/j100_0897/sensors/camera_0/color/camera_info
```

Or, if using RealSense-style topics, they may look like this:

```text
/camera/camera/color/image_raw
/camera/camera/aligned_depth_to_color/image_raw
/camera/camera/color/camera_info
```

Write down the correct topics before running the checker.

---

# 4. Confirm the camera frame

Check the image frame:

```bash
ros2 topic echo /j100_0897/sensors/camera_0/color/image --once | grep frame_id
```

If using RealSense-style topics:

```bash
ros2 topic echo /camera/camera/color/image_raw --once | grep frame_id
```

The frame may be something like:

```text
camera_color_optical_frame
```

or another robot-specific camera frame.

This frame will be used as the `fallback_source_frame` if the image message does not provide a frame.

---

# 5. Check TF connection

Check whether the robot can transform from the planner/checker target frame to the camera frame.

If the planner uses `world`:

```bash
ros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo world <camera_frame>
```

Example:

```bash
ros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo world camera_color_optical_frame
```

If that fails, try `odom`:

```bash
ros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo odom <camera_frame>
```

Example:

```bash
ros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo odom camera_color_optical_frame
```

If `odom` works but `world` does not, then run the checker with:

```bash
-p target_frame:=odom
```

If the planner must use `world`, then the system needs a valid transform such as:

```text
world → odom → base_link → camera
```

For a temporary robot test only, if `world` and `odom` are equivalent, this can be published:

```bash
ros2 run tf2_ros static_transform_publisher \
  --x 0 --y 0 --z 0 \
  --roll 0 --pitch 0 --yaw 0 \
  --frame-id world \
  --child-frame-id odom
```

Leave that running only if needed.

---

# 6. Run the placement checker

## Option A — Clearpath camera topics

Use this if the robot publishes Clearpath-style camera topics:

```bash
cd ~/aruco_ws

source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
source install/setup.bash

ros2 run manipulator placement_accuracy_checker --ros-args \
  -p color_topic:=/j100_0897/sensors/camera_0/color/image \
  -p depth_topic:=/j100_0897/sensors/camera_0/depth/image \
  -p camera_info_topic:=/j100_0897/sensors/camera_0/color/camera_info \
  -p tag_family:=16h5 \
  -p target_id:=13 \
  -p marker_size:=0.055 \
  -p target_frame:=odom \
  -p fallback_source_frame:=camera_color_optical_frame \
  -p enable_correction:=false \
  -p theta_tolerance_deg:=180.0
```

## Option B — RealSense-style topics

Use this if the robot publishes RealSense-style camera topics:

```bash
cd ~/aruco_ws

source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
source install/setup.bash

ros2 run manipulator placement_accuracy_checker --ros-args \
  -p color_topic:=/camera/camera/color/image_raw \
  -p depth_topic:=/camera/camera/aligned_depth_to_color/image_raw \
  -p camera_info_topic:=/camera/camera/color/camera_info \
  -p tag_family:=16h5 \
  -p target_id:=13 \
  -p marker_size:=0.055 \
  -p target_frame:=odom \
  -p fallback_source_frame:=camera_color_optical_frame \
  -p enable_correction:=false \
  -p theta_tolerance_deg:=180.0
```

Leave the checker running.

Expected log when the tag is visible:

```text
Tag ID 13 perceived.
Camera pose: x=..., y=..., z=...
World pose: x=..., y=..., z=...
```

The log may say “World pose,” but the actual frame is whatever `target_frame` is set to, for example `odom`.

---

# 7. View the debug image

If running with a GUI:

```bash
cd ~/aruco_ws
source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
source install/setup.bash

ros2 run rqt_image_view rqt_image_view /placement_checker/debug_image
```

If SSH-only, view this from a laptop connected to the same ROS network.

The debug image should show AprilTag ID `13` when the tag is visible.

---

# 8. Hardcoded planner service call

Since the planner is not calling the checker yet, we manually send the planner input through the service.

Always refresh the terminal before calling the service:

```bash
cd ~/aruco_ws

source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
source install/setup.bash

ros2 daemon stop
ros2 daemon start
```

Check the service:

```bash
ros2 service list | grep placement
ros2 service type /placement_checker/check_placement
```

Expected:

```text
/placement_checker/check_placement
cc_interfaces/srv/CheckPlacement
```

---

# 9. Test A — Unseen block

Move the tag out of camera view, then call:

```bash
ros2 service call /placement_checker/check_placement cc_interfaces/srv/CheckPlacement "{block_ids: ['block_13'], aruco_ids: [13], desired_poses: [{header: {frame_id: 'odom'}, pose: {position: {x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0}, orientation: {x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0, w: 1.0}}}]}"
```

Expected:

```text
unseen=['block_13']
```

If using `world` as the checker target frame, change:

```text
frame_id: 'odom'
```

to:

```text
frame_id: 'world'
```

---

# 10. Test B — Seen but misplaced

Put the tag in view, then call:

```bash
ros2 service call /placement_checker/check_placement cc_interfaces/srv/CheckPlacement "{block_ids: ['block_13'], aruco_ids: [13], desired_poses: [{header: {frame_id: 'odom'}, pose: {position: {x: 0.5, y: 0.0, z: 0.0}, orientation: {x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0, w: 1.0}}}]}"
```

Expected:

```text
misplaced=['block_13']
```

This intentionally sets the desired pose far away.

---

# 11. Test C — Properly placed

Look at the placement checker terminal and copy the current transformed pose printed in the logs:

```text
World pose: x=..., y=..., z=...
```

Use those values in the service call.

Example only:

```bash
ros2 service call /placement_checker/check_placement cc_interfaces/srv/CheckPlacement "{block_ids: ['block_13'], aruco_ids: [13], desired_poses: [{header: {frame_id: 'odom'}, pose: {position: {x: -0.033, y: 0.115, z: 0.470}, orientation: {x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0, w: 1.0}}}]}"
```

Expected:

```text
good=['block_13']
```

---

# 12. RViz check

Start RViz:

```bash
rviz2
```

Set:

```text
Fixed Frame: odom
```

or:

```text
Fixed Frame: world
```

depending on the checker `target_frame`.

Add these displays:

```text
TF
/placement_checker/marker_array
/placement_checker/debug_image
```

The checker marker array should show green for properly placed and red for misplaced.

---

# Expected Test Results

| Test                                  | Expected Result          |
| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Tag out of view                       | `unseen=['block_13']`    |
| Tag visible but desired pose far away | `misplaced=['block_13']` |
| Desired pose matches perceived pose   | `good=['block_13']`      |

---

# Current Safety Setting

Correction is disabled during this test:

```bash
-p enable_correction:=false
```

So the checker will only report the result. It will not command the arm or gripper.

Enable correction only after the perception, TF, and hardcoded checker tests are confirmed.
